{{Life in Balisca}}

'''Crime in Balisca''' is characterized by extremely low rates of violent and property crime relative to other nations in [[W:Latin America|Latin America]], a phenomenon widely attributed to the Confederation's high levels of social trust, the universal guarantee of basic necessities ([[Housing in Balisca|housing]], healthcare, food), and the structural elimination of extreme wealth inequality through the [[Equitable Society Compact]]. Balisca's historical, political, and economic evolution has greatly impacted the types and the prevalence of crime in the country. The [[Baliscan Revolution]] ushered in a [[W:Libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist]] socio-economic [[Politics of Balisca|system]], under which local and confederal administrations have focused on crime prevention through efforts such as [[W:Community policing|community policing]], [[Education in Balisca|education]], and [[Social protection in Balisca|social programs]]. Balisca has also focused on addressing the root causes of crime, such as poverty and inequality, and promoting social cohesion and solidarity among citizens.

However, Balisca is not crime-free. Criminal activity in the confederation has evolved to fit its unique regulatory environment, with law enforcement focusing heavily on tba.

